		{
			"id": "memo_group",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Memos** (11) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[S2M4]] — feedback gated by student initiative\n- [[S3M2]] — professor feedback as correctness gate\n- [[S4M3]] — shy students need private feedback channels\n- [[S5M5]] — group modelling assumes an outgoing participant\n- [[S6M4]] — pair structure and notation-specific sharing comfort\n- [[L1M6]] — two-stage assessment rewards correction\n- [[S7M4]] — initiative-gated feedback and split-by-notation peer review\n- [[S8M2]] — ungraded practice makes checking optional\n- [[S9M2]] — drawing skill and old tools shape sharing more than correctness\n- [[S10M2]] — reserved starter: notebook yes, board no\n- [[S10M4]] — AI as a distrusted classmate, not a homework generator",
			"x": -300,
			"y": 180,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 388,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "code_group",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Codes** (32) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[L1-C05]] — trial and error with tutor feedback improves models\n- [[L1-C08]] — accepts multiple valid modelling solutions\n- [[L1-C09]] — two-stage delivery with feedback on both\n- [[L1-C10]] — grades completeness consistency and readability\n- [[S2-C19]] — quality rules taught but not retained\n- [[S2-C25]] — detailed feedback only if student initiates\n- [[S2-C26]] — end-of-course feedback on models\n- [[S3-C07]] — waited for professor feedback to feel sure\n- [[S3-C13]] — picks first option when two seem valid\n- [[S3-C18]] — slides alone not enough without feedback\n- [[S4-C07]] — resolves design ambiguity privately with teacher\n- [[S4-C08]] — shy student prefers private over public model sharing\n- [[S5-C02]] — public lab presentation exposes unanswered questions\n- [[S5-C15]] — group whiteboarding suits outgoing more than quiet\n- [[S6-C06]] — documents alternative iStar decompositions with trade-offs\n- [[S6-C07]] — sharing comfort depends on notation familiarity\n- [[S6-C10]] — pair splits diagrams by type with weekly syncs\n- [[S7-C11]] — more willing to share models than code\n- [[S7-C12]] — feedback does not fall from the sky\n- [[S7-C13]] — split-by-notation then peer review for defence\n- [[S8-C02]] — ungraded exercises rarely checked unless you ask\n- [[S8-C05]] — skipped semantic tools and used draw.io\n- [[S8-C06]] — walk-around feedback almost always meant changes\n- [[S8-C07]] — shares with friends not course-group strangers\n- [[S9-C04]] — good enough when nothing is left to change\n- [[S9-C05]] — would hide poor drawing not a wrong model\n- [[S9-C06]] — archaic UML tool slower than paper but better than drawing\n- [[S10-C07]] — stays quiet at the board even with a ready solution\n- [[S10-C08]] — small public doubts go unresolved\n- [[S10-C14]] — desk walk-around beats raising a hand\n- [[S10-C11]] — AI as screenshot checker not model generator\n- [[S10-C18]] — AI cut small questions to the teacher",
			"x": 520,
			"y": 180,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 976,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "explainer",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "What this means\n\nGetting feedback depends on asking for it, on whether the work is graded, and on whether the format is public or private. Shyness, drawing skill, and dislike of the board all change who actually gets comments. Desk walk-around recovers reserved starters; raising a hand often does not.",
			"x": 80,
			"y": -320,
